#!/bin/sh
# .githooks/pre-commit — this repo's commit gate. `flow init` writes this file.
#
# Every `at(commit)` guardrail in flow.config.ts runs over the STAGED changes, and a block exits
# non-zero, which is how git refuses the commit. This fires with no agent in the room — a human
# commit, a merge, CI — which is the half of the promise the PreToolUse rail cannot make. flow asks
# git itself what the commit adds, changes and deletes, so this file passes nothing.
#
# It calls the `flow` binary on PATH, which in THIS repo is the one built from this checkout
# (`just link`). flow guarding flow is the whole dogfood: a rule that stopped loading, a door that
# stopped exporting, or a bundle that stopped building refuses this repo's own next commit first.
#
# Wired by `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`, which is per-CLONE and not committed — every
# fresh clone and every worktree needs it again, and `flow status` says so when it is missing.

exec flow hook commit
